Title:
GDRVLoader – Load Unsigned Drivers Windows 10–11 (CVE-2018-19320)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
GDRVLoader is a Windows user-mode loader that abuses a vulnerable signed driver (Gigabyte GDRV) to load and unload arbitrary unsigned kernel drivers on Windows 10/11. It targets the common obstacle of Driver Signature Enforcement by leveraging CVE-2018-19320 to gain kernel read/write primitives via the vulnerable driver’s IOCTL interface. The project is based on an earlier gdrv-loader implementation but adds portability improvements (embedding/byte-loading the vulnerable driver) and a simplified CLI workflow. It also claims compatibility with newer Windows versions and aims to avoid PatchGuard-related issues during the loading process. This is primarily useful for red teamers, exploit developers, and kernel security researchers who need to test unsigned drivers or kernel payloads in realistic environments. It’s notable because it operationalizes a known BYOVD technique into a straightforward loader for modern Windows builds.

Technical Focus:
- BYOVD (Bring Your Own Vulnerable Driver) for kernel access
- CVE-2018-19320 (Gigabyte GDRV.sys) exploitation via IOCTLs
- Kernel-mode driver loading/unloading from user mode
- Windows 10/11 Driver Signature Enforcement (DSE) bypass patterns
- PatchGuard considerations when manipulating kernel state

Use Cases:
- Load a custom unsigned kernel driver for security research or red team operations
- Rapidly test kernel callbacks/hooks/telemetry evasion concepts in lab environments
- Prototype kernel exploitation chains that require a signed vulnerable driver foothold
- Unload/reload drivers during iterative driver development and debugging

Keywords:
GDRVLoader, BYOVD, vulnerable driver, Gigabyte, GDRV.sys, CVE-2018-19320, IOCTL, kernel read/write, unsigned driver, DSE bypass, Windows 10, Windows 11, PatchGuard, driver loading, Service Control Manager, kernel-mode, EDR evasion, privilege escalation, Windows internals, red team tooling